{
  "component": "Search",
  "description": "A single-line search field with a leading search icon, offered in eight variants across four states and two value types. The Value Type property switches between placeholder text and an entered value, and the State property covers Default, Hover, Focus and Disabled. PURPOSE: Lets people type a query to filter or find content. The pill shape (Radius/Full) and the icon mark it as a search field rather than a general text input. BEHAVIOUR: Hover changes the field's surface and border. Focus applies the Stroke/Focus Ring border and Border/Focus/Default colour. Disabled swaps background, border, text and icon to the disabled token set and blocks input. Value Type=Placeholder uses Text/Default/Tertiary for the prompt text; Value Type=Filled uses Text/Default/Default for real content. COMPOSITION: Two nested instances carry the icon. Placeholder variants use an instance named 'Search'; filled variants use an instance named 'container'. Both wrap a single Vector icon layer. AI code generators should look for these icon sub-components in the development codebase before building new ones. USAGE: Use this for query entry in toolbars, page headers, list filters and pickers. Use a standard text field when the input is not a search, and a select or combobox when the user picks from a fixed set. CODE GENERATION NOTES: Render as input[type=search] with an accessible name, since there is no visible label layer. The Value text layer maps to the value or placeholder attribute depending on Value Type. Hover and Focus are browser states, not separate components, so build one element with :hover and :focus-visible rather than four. The focus ring should sit outside the border so it stays visible on coloured backgrounds. Two icon instance names exist for the same role, so consolidate to one in code. Debounce input before firing a query, and wire Enter to submit.",
